1
00:00:01,335 --> 00:00:02,997
♪♪

2
00:02:11,798 --> 00:02:14,165
Cold, that's how it was.

3
00:02:14,301 --> 00:02:16,031
Cold and wet.

4
00:02:16,169 --> 00:02:18,104
Why anybody would
want to be President

5
00:02:18,238 --> 00:02:21,675
and live in Washington, D.C.
for four years is beyond me.

6
00:02:21,808 --> 00:02:25,108
Now, they say he's a cross
between a liberal and a conservative.

7
00:02:25,245 --> 00:02:27,476
I heard somebody
call him a "libercon."

8
00:02:27,614 --> 00:02:29,981
<i>A libercon? How
do you spell loser?</i>

9
00:02:30,117 --> 00:02:32,814
<i>Yeah. Listen, I'll
see you Tuesday.</i>

10
00:02:32,953 --> 00:02:34,230
<i>- That will be fine.
- All right.</i>

11
00:02:34,254 --> 00:02:35,722
<i>I'll see you then.</i>

12
00:02:36,723 --> 00:02:38,988
The police chief's on line 1.

13
00:02:39,126 --> 00:02:41,721
The town engineer,
Lauren Brinkman, he's on 2.

14
00:02:41,862 --> 00:02:44,058
And there's a John Smith on 3.

15
00:02:44,197 --> 00:02:46,325
He says he's
returning your call.

16
00:02:46,466 --> 00:02:49,925
Well, tell the chief and
Mr. Brinkman I'll be right with them.

17
00:02:54,841 --> 00:02:56,639
This is the mayor.

18
00:02:56,777 --> 00:02:58,507
<i>When did you get back in town?</i>

19
00:02:58,645 --> 00:03:01,376
Just now. Have you got anything?

20
00:03:01,515 --> 00:03:03,313
<i>Yeah, but not much.</i>

21
00:03:03,450 --> 00:03:06,614
You know the Shun Lee
Cafe at 8th and Lee in Atlanta?

22
00:03:06,753 --> 00:03:07,914
Yeah.

23
00:03:08,055 --> 00:03:11,457
<i>Meet me there tomorrow
afternoon at 3:00.</i>

24
00:03:11,591 --> 00:03:13,253
But I don't have any proof.

25
00:03:13,393 --> 00:03:16,124
<i>Well, let's talk anyway.</i>

26
00:04:00,907 --> 00:04:04,309
I'm telling you, Dwight, that's
the mayor of Willow Springs.

27
00:04:04,444 --> 00:04:06,072
I saw his picture in the paper.

28
00:04:06,213 --> 00:04:08,444
- He just got back from Washington.
- Uh-huh.

29
00:04:08,582 --> 00:04:11,950
Isn't it wonderful, honey?
He's just like plain folks.

30
00:04:12,085 --> 00:04:13,085
And I like that.

31
00:04:13,220 --> 00:04:15,849
Dwight, get your camera.

32
00:04:15,989 --> 00:04:17,617
Oh, Mona.

33
00:04:17,758 --> 00:04:19,693
Honey, come on.

34
00:04:19,826 --> 00:04:21,226
Okay.

35
00:04:24,364 --> 00:04:26,356
Zoom in. Zoom in.

36
00:04:50,390 --> 00:04:51,915
It's the mayor!

37
00:05:04,838 --> 00:05:06,500
Move it! Move it, please!

38
00:05:06,640 --> 00:05:09,075
Everybody, get back. Just
move it. Come on, buddy.

39
00:05:14,915 --> 00:05:17,316
Move it back! Move
it back, please.

40
00:05:26,126 --> 00:05:28,755
Hey, he has a
gun! He's got a gun!

41
00:05:36,636 --> 00:05:38,730
- Hands behind the back.
- All right.

42
00:05:38,872 --> 00:05:40,738
Spread eagle. Come on.

43
00:05:40,874 --> 00:05:42,399
Cuff this guy, will you?

44
00:05:43,476 --> 00:05:44,842
Come on!

45
00:05:53,086 --> 00:05:55,385
He was caught right
there at the scene, Arthur.

46
00:05:55,522 --> 00:05:58,390
His rifle. His bullets.
His fingerprints.

47
00:05:58,525 --> 00:06:01,256
Hell, he even confessed.
What more do you want?

48
00:06:01,394 --> 00:06:03,506
The man who paid him to pull
the trigger. That's what I want.

49
00:06:03,530 --> 00:06:06,227
If somebody paid him 25 grand,
like he claims, what happened to it?

50
00:06:06,366 --> 00:06:08,426
It wasn't in his car. It
wasn't in his apartment.

51
00:06:08,568 --> 00:06:09,592
It wasn't in his bank.

52
00:06:09,736 --> 00:06:11,080
I think somebody
stole it back from him.

53
00:06:11,104 --> 00:06:14,006
I think somebody's trying to make
it look like this man acted alone,

54
00:06:14,140 --> 00:06:15,665
when, in fact, it
was a conspiracy.

55
00:06:15,809 --> 00:06:20,179
Damn it, Arthur, this commission
was convened three weeks ago.

56
00:06:20,313 --> 00:06:24,307
We've all assembled in this room
for four hours a day, five days a week.

57
00:06:24,451 --> 00:06:27,046
We brought in Detective Bob
Brooks from the Atlanta P.D.

58
00:06:27,187 --> 00:06:29,383
to conduct an
independent investigation.

59
00:06:29,522 --> 00:06:31,252
We've all heard
the same testimony.

60
00:06:31,391 --> 00:06:32,831
We've gone over
the same evidence...

61
00:06:32,959 --> 00:06:36,555
You, me, the police chief there,
the director of St. Luke's Hospital,

62
00:06:36,696 --> 00:06:38,665
the head of the League
of American Voters.

63
00:06:38,798 --> 00:06:39,925
We all know this.

64
00:06:40,066 --> 00:06:43,066
Then why are you the only one who
believes this cockamamie conspiracy thing?

65
00:06:43,169 --> 00:06:44,169
Because I do.

66
00:06:44,237 --> 00:06:46,900
Jay Reynolds was a
disgruntled ex-city employee

67
00:06:47,040 --> 00:06:49,040
who believed it was the
mayor's fault he was fired.

68
00:06:49,109 --> 00:06:51,203
First, he sent him
letters, then he shot him.

69
00:06:51,344 --> 00:06:53,256
We found the typewriter he
used right there in his room.

70
00:06:53,280 --> 00:06:54,280
Okay. Fine, fine.

71
00:06:54,347 --> 00:06:56,893
How did he know that the mayor was
going to be at Shun Lee Cafe that day?

72
00:06:56,917 --> 00:06:57,976
He was stalking him.

73
00:06:58,118 --> 00:07:01,611
We've established that it would have
taken Jay Reynolds at least two minutes

74
00:07:01,755 --> 00:07:03,690
to get up to the roof
to assemble that rifle.

75
00:07:03,823 --> 00:07:04,967
Yes, Arthur, but
you can't deny...

76
00:07:04,991 --> 00:07:08,325
Why is it, then, that everyone
who was at the cafe that day

77
00:07:08,461 --> 00:07:10,430
says the mayor was there
for less than one minute?

78
00:07:10,563 --> 00:07:11,997
- Arthur...
- No, wait a minute.

79
00:07:12,132 --> 00:07:14,624
But if someone had
tipped off Jay Reynolds...

80
00:07:14,768 --> 00:07:16,327
Which is what the
man contends...

81
00:07:16,469 --> 00:07:18,836
He would have
been up there waiting.

82
00:07:18,972 --> 00:07:22,238
Maybe everybody in that
cafe was just plain wrong.

83
00:07:22,375 --> 00:07:25,573
Maybe Jay Reynolds overheard the
mayor telling somebody where he was going.

84
00:07:25,712 --> 00:07:26,712
Maybe 100 other things.

85
00:07:26,846 --> 00:07:30,977
Why are you so eager to believe the
rantings and ravings of a confessed killer?

86
00:07:31,117 --> 00:07:36,351
Because I am not sure, Leo, that
justice is being served. That's why.

87
00:07:47,100 --> 00:07:49,092
Are you missing
something, Counselor?

88
00:07:49,235 --> 00:07:53,070
Uh, my client's in Denver.

89
00:07:53,206 --> 00:07:56,472
He went there on an
emergency and got snowed in.

90
00:07:56,609 --> 00:07:58,339
This is the third
time in eight days.

91
00:07:58,478 --> 00:08:00,003
He went there on business.

92
00:08:00,146 --> 00:08:02,138
What's he to do, Your Honor?

93
00:08:02,282 --> 00:08:04,774
We'd like another continuance.

94
00:08:06,920 --> 00:08:08,013
No.

95
00:08:09,356 --> 00:08:10,847
Excuse me?

96
00:08:10,991 --> 00:08:12,468
I'm not going to grant
you a continuance,

97
00:08:12,492 --> 00:08:15,485
and I'm going to charge your
client with failure to appear.

98
00:08:15,628 --> 00:08:18,757
And I'm going to issue a no-bail
bench warrant for his arrest

99
00:08:18,898 --> 00:08:21,210
so that the next time he shows
his face in this jurisdiction,

100
00:08:21,234 --> 00:08:26,229
he will be arrested and incarcerated,
and his trail will begin forthwith.

101
00:08:26,373 --> 00:08:27,373
Thank you.

102
00:08:27,440 --> 00:08:28,931
You can't do that.

103
00:08:29,075 --> 00:08:31,203
I know this is just Willow
Springs, Mr. Matlock,

104
00:08:31,344 --> 00:08:34,109
but I prefer that you don't tell
me what I can and cannot do.

105
00:08:34,247 --> 00:08:35,408
I am not in the mood.

106
00:08:35,548 --> 00:08:39,349
That is the dumbest, most
pig-headed ruling I've ever heard of.

107
00:08:39,486 --> 00:08:40,763
Did you call me
pig-headed and dumb?

108
00:08:40,787 --> 00:08:42,153
That's what I
called your ruling.

109
00:08:42,288 --> 00:08:43,288
Bailiff.

110
00:08:43,356 --> 00:08:45,134
I hope you don't have
a lot of plans for tonight

111
00:08:45,158 --> 00:08:49,254
because you, sir, are going
to be invited to a slumber party.

112
00:08:49,396 --> 00:08:51,695
Oh, no, Judge. Give me
a break. Give me a break.

113
00:08:51,831 --> 00:08:54,665
I've got somebody coming to town
that I haven't seen in a long time.

114
00:08:54,801 --> 00:08:56,497
- Pig-headed and dumb, huh?
- Yeah. Yeah!

115
00:08:56,636 --> 00:08:59,299
Dumb. Pig-headed.
Pig-headed. Dumb.

116
00:08:59,439 --> 00:09:02,932
Pig, dumb-headed. Pig-headed.
Get your thumb out of my arm.

117
00:09:55,628 --> 00:09:58,154
I was hoping to surprise you.

118
00:10:06,039 --> 00:10:08,270
It's been a rotten day.

119
00:10:08,408 --> 00:10:11,344
Do you want to
talk about it? Hmm?

120
00:10:11,478 --> 00:10:13,743
No. I think I'd rather do this.

121
00:10:43,309 --> 00:10:44,309
Judge Eller?

122
00:10:44,444 --> 00:10:45,444
Yeah.

123
00:10:45,545 --> 00:10:47,173
Sir, we have a
warrant for your arrest.

124
00:10:47,313 --> 00:10:48,872
What charge?

125
00:10:49,015 --> 00:10:50,813
Attempted sexual assault.

126
00:10:50,950 --> 00:10:53,010
I'm sorry, sir. Step
outside, please.

127
00:11:21,981 --> 00:11:23,506
Hi, Dad.

128
00:11:30,056 --> 00:11:31,183
Hi.

129
00:11:32,292 --> 00:11:34,454
You know, this could
get me on <i>Donahue.</i>

130
00:11:34,594 --> 00:11:36,961
That's not funny, Leigh Anne.

131
00:11:37,096 --> 00:11:40,931
Doing hard time in the big house
has just ruined your sense of humor.

132
00:11:44,270 --> 00:11:47,263
Which judge did you
call a jackass this time?

133
00:11:47,407 --> 00:11:49,808
I didn't exactly
call him a jackass.

134
00:11:49,943 --> 00:11:52,242
- I just...
- Lost your temper.

135
00:11:52,378 --> 00:11:55,007
Well, he is a jackass.

136
00:11:55,148 --> 00:11:57,276
I don't want to get into it.

137
00:11:57,417 --> 00:11:59,257
- You shouldn't call him a jackass.
- I didn't.

138
00:12:03,089 --> 00:12:05,854
I bet you've spent more
time in jail for contempt

139
00:12:05,992 --> 00:12:07,672
than most of your
clients have for murder.

140
00:12:07,760 --> 00:12:09,626
Why did you let her in here?

141
00:12:09,762 --> 00:12:11,094
I used to work here, Dad.

142
00:12:11,231 --> 00:12:16,260
Some people still know me,
and they were happy to see me.

143
00:12:18,137 --> 00:12:19,196
Well...

144
00:12:22,408 --> 00:12:24,036
I am, too.

145
00:12:26,779 --> 00:12:30,910
And I'm happy to be here, Dad.

146
00:12:48,968 --> 00:12:52,166
Wow. You look great.

147
00:12:52,305 --> 00:12:53,329
Healthy.

148
00:12:54,741 --> 00:12:56,266
You, too.

149
00:12:57,777 --> 00:12:59,575
How is your husband?

150
00:12:59,712 --> 00:13:02,113
Busy, and I'm not,

151
00:13:02,248 --> 00:13:04,683
So I thought I'd come home
for a few days and visit you.

152
00:13:04,817 --> 00:13:10,188
That's good.

153
00:13:10,323 --> 00:13:12,019
Really good?

154
00:13:13,893 --> 00:13:16,328
Yeah. Yeah.

155
00:13:18,031 --> 00:13:19,829
I'll get out tomorrow at 8,

156
00:13:19,966 --> 00:13:24,267
and I'll take you to
Brendan's for breakfast.

157
00:13:24,404 --> 00:13:27,431
We'll have corncakes
and sausage and stuff.

158
00:13:27,573 --> 00:13:30,008
I'll pick you up at 8.

159
00:13:31,944 --> 00:13:35,142
Keep an eye on him,
Frank. He looks dangerous.

160
00:13:52,465 --> 00:13:53,933
Hi.

161
00:13:54,067 --> 00:13:55,558
What's this all about?

162
00:13:55,702 --> 00:13:58,638
It's a party to welcome
you home from jail.

163
00:13:58,771 --> 00:14:01,138
And to meet you.

164
00:14:01,274 --> 00:14:03,300
How are you? Conrad McMasters.

165
00:14:03,443 --> 00:14:05,537
- Leigh Anne Mclntyre.
- Leigh Anne, hi.

166
00:14:05,678 --> 00:14:08,011
- I'm Julie.
- Hi, Julie. Leigh Anne.

167
00:14:08,147 --> 00:14:09,171
Hi.

168
00:14:10,983 --> 00:14:13,145
Well, it's nice to
meet all of you.

169
00:14:13,286 --> 00:14:15,448
It's nice to meet you, too.

170
00:14:15,588 --> 00:14:17,580
Ben hasn't told
us a lot about you.

171
00:14:17,724 --> 00:14:19,386
He hasn't?

172
00:14:19,525 --> 00:14:23,986
Well, we know you're his daughter
and you're married and you're a lawyer.

173
00:14:24,130 --> 00:14:26,531
You're sure pretty.

174
00:14:26,666 --> 00:14:27,827
Thank you.

175
00:14:27,967 --> 00:14:30,402
Like I was saying, there's
nothing like a pretty girl.

176
00:14:34,040 --> 00:14:36,202
So, Leigh Anne, do
you have any children?

177
00:14:36,342 --> 00:14:39,471
- No, I don't.
- Oh.

178
00:14:39,612 --> 00:14:41,308
That's too bad.

179
00:14:46,786 --> 00:14:50,985
Well, come on, everybody.
It's time to risk your lives.

180
00:14:51,124 --> 00:14:52,888
I've made breakfast.

181
00:14:54,394 --> 00:14:56,761
I don't think I
can eat anything.

182
00:14:56,896 --> 00:14:59,559
I've had corncakes
and eggs and bacon.

183
00:14:59,699 --> 00:15:01,463
I just hate that:

184
00:15:01,601 --> 00:15:04,594
"You don't have children?
Oh, what a shame.

185
00:15:04,737 --> 00:15:06,865
When are you going to have one?"

186
00:15:07,006 --> 00:15:08,668
When are you?

187
00:15:08,808 --> 00:15:10,743
Dad.

188
00:15:10,877 --> 00:15:13,142
Nothing's changed.

189
00:15:13,279 --> 00:15:14,804
Don't get mad at me.

190
00:15:14,947 --> 00:15:15,947
I won't.

191
00:15:16,048 --> 00:15:18,745
It's just that things
like babies are personal

192
00:15:18,885 --> 00:15:21,514
and should remain
just that... personal.

193
00:15:21,654 --> 00:15:24,783
Yeah.

194
00:15:24,924 --> 00:15:27,894
I'm going to look
around the old house.

195
00:15:28,027 --> 00:15:30,428
Nothing's changed.

196
00:15:30,563 --> 00:15:33,032
You haven't heard what
happened to Judge Eller, have you?

197
00:15:34,033 --> 00:15:35,057
No.

198
00:15:35,201 --> 00:15:36,999
He got arrested last night.

199
00:15:37,136 --> 00:15:38,365
What? What?

200
00:15:38,504 --> 00:15:40,837
Some woman accused
him of assaulting her.

201
00:15:40,973 --> 00:15:42,805
He hit a woman?

202
00:15:42,942 --> 00:15:44,069
Assault.

203
00:15:46,245 --> 00:15:47,338
Rape?

204
00:15:48,548 --> 00:15:51,017
Oh.

205
00:15:51,150 --> 00:15:53,210
He's a known womanizer,

206
00:15:53,352 --> 00:15:56,914
but Jackass went too
far that time, didn't he?

207
00:16:07,366 --> 00:16:10,530
Ah. So you're Leigh Anne.

208
00:16:10,670 --> 00:16:12,229
Yep, I'm Leigh Anne.

209
00:16:12,371 --> 00:16:14,441
And you're... Les.

210
00:16:14,574 --> 00:16:15,598
Coffee?

211
00:16:15,741 --> 00:16:17,004
Just a tad.

212
00:16:17,143 --> 00:16:22,514
Not too much now. I've got
to take care of the old bod.

213
00:16:22,648 --> 00:16:24,241
So you're a married lady.

214
00:16:24,383 --> 00:16:25,476
That's right.

215
00:16:25,618 --> 00:16:27,052
You like it? You happy?

216
00:16:27,186 --> 00:16:29,121
Well, yeah.

217
00:16:29,255 --> 00:16:31,121
Ups and downs, you know.

218
00:16:31,257 --> 00:16:33,089
Well, I know ups and downs.

219
00:16:33,226 --> 00:16:34,888
I was married once.

220
00:16:35,027 --> 00:16:37,519
She's gone now.

221
00:16:37,663 --> 00:16:39,063
Oh, I'm sorry.

222
00:16:39,198 --> 00:16:40,393
Oh, she didn't die.

223
00:16:40,533 --> 00:16:42,764
She ran off with a
drummer from Ohio.

224
00:16:42,902 --> 00:16:44,097
Oh.

225
00:16:44,237 --> 00:16:48,197
He sold hats and scarves,
overshoes and stuff like that.

226
00:16:48,341 --> 00:16:49,934
Interesting.

227
00:16:50,076 --> 00:16:53,513
So you don't have any children.

228
00:16:54,947 --> 00:16:56,040
No, Les, I don't.

229
00:16:56,182 --> 00:17:02,554
Oh, that's a shame... Someone
to carry on, look after you.

230
00:17:02,688 --> 00:17:05,522
You know, Ben never
talked about you much.

231
00:17:05,658 --> 00:17:07,854
Well, maybe he didn't
have anything to say.

232
00:17:07,994 --> 00:17:09,724
Well, maybe not.

233
00:17:09,862 --> 00:17:12,195
You know, you ought to
spend more time with him.

234
00:17:12,331 --> 00:17:13,891
And you ought to
give him a grandchild.

235
00:17:13,966 --> 00:17:15,525
A man needs that.

236
00:17:15,668 --> 00:17:17,864
Yes, I suppose so.

237
00:17:18,004 --> 00:17:20,405
Do you have children
and grandchildren, Les?

238
00:17:20,540 --> 00:17:21,803
No.

239
00:17:21,941 --> 00:17:23,375
The wife left.

240
00:17:23,509 --> 00:17:26,877
With that drummer who sold hats
and scarves and overshoes and stuff.

241
00:17:27,013 --> 00:17:29,209
- Do you date, Les?
- Me? Oh, sure.

242
00:17:29,348 --> 00:17:32,147
I'm out there among 'em,
two, three nights a week.

243
00:17:32,285 --> 00:17:33,309
Any regulars?

244
00:17:33,452 --> 00:17:35,080
I've got a little black book.

245
00:17:35,221 --> 00:17:36,951
Ladies your own age?

246
00:17:37,089 --> 00:17:39,183
Yeah. There's this
one, she's my age.

247
00:17:39,325 --> 00:17:40,452
How old is that?

248
00:17:40,593 --> 00:17:42,289
Early 60s. Right in the prime.

249
00:17:42,428 --> 00:17:44,624
Prime's the time.

250
00:17:44,764 --> 00:17:47,700
I don't see her
that much, though.

251
00:17:47,833 --> 00:17:49,233
What do you talk about?

252
00:17:49,368 --> 00:17:52,827
Oh, different things:
cemetery plots.

253
00:17:52,972 --> 00:17:54,941
Do you have any younger ones?

254
00:17:55,074 --> 00:17:56,440
Sure.

255
00:17:56,576 --> 00:17:58,306
I've got one in her 30s.

256
00:17:58,444 --> 00:17:59,444
Thirty what?

257
00:17:59,579 --> 00:18:00,774
- Well, thirty, uh...
- What?

258
00:18:00,913 --> 00:18:02,245
39.

259
00:18:02,381 --> 00:18:04,509
Now we're talking.
What do you do with her?

260
00:18:04,650 --> 00:18:07,620
Well, we bowl, play
ping pong, miniature golf.

261
00:18:07,753 --> 00:18:08,777
All kinds of things.

262
00:18:08,921 --> 00:18:11,823
But when it gets dark and you can't
do those things, then what do you do?

263
00:18:11,958 --> 00:18:13,824
We go back to her house.

264
00:18:13,960 --> 00:18:15,895
- And?
- And what?

265
00:18:16,028 --> 00:18:19,624
Do you and this 39-year-old
get down to business?

266
00:18:19,765 --> 00:18:21,645
I don't know what you're
getting at, young lady.

267
00:18:21,734 --> 00:18:22,861
That's too personal.

268
00:18:23,002 --> 00:18:25,322
And you just wait and see...
I'm going to tell your father.

269
00:18:35,314 --> 00:18:37,283
I'm sorry about what
happened yesterday.

270
00:18:37,416 --> 00:18:39,612
I was wrong, and I
apologize. I was out of line.

271
00:18:42,989 --> 00:18:44,981
The commission I'm
on, it's making me crazy.

272
00:18:45,124 --> 00:18:48,652
The way I hear it, what
went around came around.

273
00:18:48,794 --> 00:18:50,092
Is that right?

274
00:18:50,229 --> 00:18:51,424
I'm innocent, I swear.

275
00:18:55,234 --> 00:18:57,499
You want me to defend you.

276
00:18:59,338 --> 00:19:00,772
No way.

277
00:19:00,906 --> 00:19:02,204
- Get out.
- Hello.

278
00:19:03,776 --> 00:19:06,302
This is the jackass
who threw me in jail.

279
00:19:06,445 --> 00:19:08,505
Ben, I'm innocent.
Just give me a minute.

280
00:19:08,648 --> 00:19:10,674
Hear my side of it, please.

281
00:19:10,816 --> 00:19:15,516
The best lawyer I ever knew once told
me to put truth beyond everything else.

282
00:19:15,655 --> 00:19:18,523
He said, "Principles
before personalities."

283
00:19:19,692 --> 00:19:21,627
Did I get that right, Dad?

284
00:19:26,599 --> 00:19:27,897
Come on in.

285
00:19:35,007 --> 00:19:36,407
Jerk.

286
00:19:37,710 --> 00:19:40,009
I met her at a party
two weeks ago.

287
00:19:40,146 --> 00:19:41,146
Mm-hmm.

288
00:19:41,247 --> 00:19:42,791
We talked. We did
exchange phone numbers.

289
00:19:42,815 --> 00:19:44,010
- But that was it.
- Yeah.

290
00:19:44,150 --> 00:19:46,070
The next thing I know,
the police are at the door

291
00:19:46,118 --> 00:19:49,577
saying that she's accusing me of
having attacked her two nights ago.

292
00:19:49,722 --> 00:19:51,520
I never even phoned her.

293
00:19:51,657 --> 00:19:53,455
Why would she lie, Judge Eller?

294
00:19:53,592 --> 00:19:57,051
Yeah, Leigh Anne,
why would she lie?

295
00:19:57,196 --> 00:19:58,892
Leigh Anne Matlock!

296
00:19:59,031 --> 00:20:01,057
Of course. That's why
you look so familiar.

297
00:20:01,200 --> 00:20:02,668
You used to be
a prosecutor here.

298
00:20:02,802 --> 00:20:05,499
Actually, my last name is
Leigh Anne Mclntyre now.

299
00:20:05,638 --> 00:20:07,197
I'm a prosecutor
in Philadelphia.

300
00:20:07,339 --> 00:20:08,830
Why would she lie, Judge?

301
00:20:08,974 --> 00:20:10,169
I don't know.

302
00:20:10,309 --> 00:20:11,868
Maybe she's a fruitcake.

303
00:20:12,011 --> 00:20:13,689
You're not going to believe
what she said I did to her.

304
00:20:13,713 --> 00:20:15,511
Try me.

305
00:20:15,648 --> 00:20:19,813
According to her, I invite
her up to my place for a drink,

306
00:20:19,952 --> 00:20:23,946
excuse myself, return five
minutes later dressed like a cowboy.

307
00:20:24,090 --> 00:20:27,151
Then I tied her up, and
I attempted to rape her.

308
00:20:27,293 --> 00:20:28,852
That's bizarre.

309
00:20:28,994 --> 00:20:30,189
And it's a lie.

310
00:20:35,201 --> 00:20:37,193
Why are you looking at me?

311
00:20:37,336 --> 00:20:39,464
You throw me in jail,
you could do anything.

312
00:20:39,605 --> 00:20:40,629
Maybe.

313
00:20:40,773 --> 00:20:42,742
But I didn't assault that woman.

314
00:20:42,875 --> 00:20:46,403
I don't know, maybe she hates
men. Maybe she hates judges.

315
00:20:46,545 --> 00:20:50,641
I can't give you a definitive
answer. I'm not a psychiatrist.

316
00:20:50,783 --> 00:20:53,116
A psychiatrist will be
our first expert witness.

317
00:20:53,252 --> 00:20:54,252
Ours?

318
00:20:54,386 --> 00:20:56,184
Where were you on
the night in question?

319
00:20:56,322 --> 00:20:58,120
I have a place
up at Athens Lake.

320
00:20:58,257 --> 00:21:01,694
I often go there right
after work... alone.

321
00:21:02,962 --> 00:21:04,396
Could anyone have
seen you there?

322
00:21:04,530 --> 00:21:06,362
Not that I know of.

323
00:21:06,499 --> 00:21:08,764
I took a walk on the beach.

324
00:21:08,901 --> 00:21:10,995
Somebody might
have seen me there.

325
00:21:11,137 --> 00:21:14,005
Ben, please, will
you take my case?

326
00:21:14,140 --> 00:21:16,132
When hell freezes over.

327
00:21:17,209 --> 00:21:19,041
Please excuse us.

328
00:21:20,412 --> 00:21:21,412
Dad.

329
00:21:29,088 --> 00:21:30,784
Now don't start up on me.

330
00:21:30,923 --> 00:21:32,721
He could have done it.
He could have done it.

331
00:21:32,858 --> 00:21:33,951
He put me in jail.

332
00:21:34,093 --> 00:21:36,493
Under the law, he's entitled
to the best defense he can find.

333
00:21:36,595 --> 00:21:38,564
His case has merit,
Dad, and you know it.

334
00:21:38,697 --> 00:21:41,292
I don't know, Leigh Anne.
The man's a womanizer.

335
00:21:41,433 --> 00:21:43,334
Well, you could give him a shot.

336
00:21:43,469 --> 00:21:48,498
All those lectures you gave me about
the law: principles before personalities.

337
00:21:48,641 --> 00:21:51,201
Dad, we could work together.

338
00:21:51,343 --> 00:21:53,403
Come on, put up or shut up.

339
00:21:56,248 --> 00:21:58,479
Do you like this guy?

340
00:21:58,617 --> 00:22:00,518
Dad, I don't even know him.

341
00:22:00,653 --> 00:22:02,485
But what if she's
setting him up?

342
00:22:02,621 --> 00:22:05,022
Let's give it a shot.

343
00:22:05,157 --> 00:22:09,060
Oh, I don't know, Leigh
Anne. I don't... I just don't know.

344
00:22:09,195 --> 00:22:11,790
My heart's not in it.

345
00:22:44,230 --> 00:22:46,141
- I'm sorry, you guys.
- What do you think you're doing?

346
00:22:46,165 --> 00:22:49,363
I'm sorry. I just wondered if you
happened to be here three nights ago.

347
00:22:49,501 --> 00:22:51,902
What is this? A
survey? Get lost.

348
00:22:52,037 --> 00:22:54,336
Wait. I really need an answer.

349
00:23:06,819 --> 00:23:09,983
I met him at a party that a
friend of mine was throwing

350
00:23:10,122 --> 00:23:12,717
for somebody who's
running for Congress.

351
00:23:12,858 --> 00:23:16,852
He wasn't with anybody, and
neither was I, so we talked.

352
00:23:18,063 --> 00:23:20,862
I gave him my phone
number, and he gave me his.

353
00:23:21,000 --> 00:23:22,161
And a couple of weeks later,

354
00:23:22,301 --> 00:23:24,770
he called me and asked me
to come to his place for a drink.

355
00:23:24,904 --> 00:23:27,533
And you went? Why?
You barely knew the man.

356
00:23:27,673 --> 00:23:30,142
Well, he was a
judge, for Pete's sake.

357
00:23:30,276 --> 00:23:32,541
I mean, how was I supposed
to know he was a pervert?

358
00:23:32,678 --> 00:23:34,112
Move to strike.

359
00:23:34,246 --> 00:23:38,240
So ordered. The jury will
disregard the witness' last remark.

360
00:23:40,085 --> 00:23:42,680
What happened when you went
to Judge Eller's house, Lydia?

361
00:23:42,821 --> 00:23:47,623
We sat and had a glass of wine
and talked for about a half hour.

362
00:23:47,760 --> 00:23:48,921
It was very nice.

363
00:23:49,061 --> 00:23:50,893
But then he excused himself,

364
00:23:51,030 --> 00:23:53,761
and when he came back
a couple of minutes later,

365
00:23:53,899 --> 00:23:58,303
he was dressed up like a cowboy,
but he wasn't wearing any pants.

366
00:23:58,437 --> 00:24:02,966
I mean, he had the guns,
the lasso, everything else.

367
00:24:03,108 --> 00:24:04,872
This is very difficult.

368
00:24:05,010 --> 00:24:08,412
I know this is
difficult, Miss Lewan.

369
00:24:08,547 --> 00:24:09,810
What happened next?

370
00:24:11,317 --> 00:24:14,981
The next thing I knew, he had the
rope around me and was tying me up.

371
00:24:15,120 --> 00:24:16,986
And then he
started to attack me.

372
00:24:17,122 --> 00:24:18,988
I struggled so
hard to fight him off

373
00:24:19,124 --> 00:24:22,492
that the rope came loose
and I managed to get free.

374
00:24:22,628 --> 00:24:25,928
And then I kicked
him where it hurts,

375
00:24:26,065 --> 00:24:29,467
and I ran out to my car as
fast as I could and drove off.

376
00:24:30,736 --> 00:24:32,034
Thank you, Lydia.

377
00:24:33,806 --> 00:24:35,297
No further questions,
Your Honor.

378
00:24:35,441 --> 00:24:36,909
Mr. Matlock?

379
00:24:37,042 --> 00:24:39,807
Just one question, Miss Lewan.

380
00:24:39,945 --> 00:24:44,144
The events you just described,
when exactly did they take place?

381
00:24:44,283 --> 00:24:47,776
On April 3rd, at
around 9 in the evening.

382
00:24:47,920 --> 00:24:49,582
Thank you.

383
00:24:49,722 --> 00:24:51,082
The prosecution
rests, Your Honor.

384
00:24:51,123 --> 00:24:53,115
You may step down.

385
00:24:53,258 --> 00:24:54,624
Thank you.

386
00:24:54,760 --> 00:24:57,491
Mr. Matlock, you may
call your first witness.

387
00:24:57,629 --> 00:25:01,031
Defense calls Mr. Edward
Price to the stand.

388
00:25:07,806 --> 00:25:12,767
Where were you between 9 and 10
the evening of April the 3rd, Eddie?

389
00:25:12,911 --> 00:25:14,607
I was at Athens Lake.

390
00:25:14,747 --> 00:25:15,908
Doing what?

391
00:25:17,316 --> 00:25:20,184
Me and my girl were in my car...

392
00:25:20,319 --> 00:25:22,413
Well, you know, making out.

393
00:25:22,554 --> 00:25:23,647
Oh.

394
00:25:24,890 --> 00:25:30,522
Did you seen anybody at the lake that
night that you see in the courtroom today?

395
00:25:30,662 --> 00:25:32,597
I saw him.

396
00:25:32,731 --> 00:25:36,463
Let the record show that the
witness is pointing to the defendant.

397
00:25:36,602 --> 00:25:38,901
You're sure you saw Judge Eller?

398
00:25:39,038 --> 00:25:40,438
He was walking on the beach.

399
00:25:40,572 --> 00:25:43,132
There was a full moon out, so
I could see his face real good.

400
00:25:43,275 --> 00:25:45,904
I knew who he was because he's
a friend of my girlfriend's father.

401
00:25:46,045 --> 00:25:51,677
How long does it take to drive
from Willow Springs to Athens Lake?

402
00:25:51,817 --> 00:25:53,649
About two hours.

403
00:25:53,786 --> 00:25:57,086
So if a person left
Willow Springs at 9:00,

404
00:25:57,222 --> 00:26:00,954
there's no way he could
get to Athens Lake by 10?

405
00:26:01,093 --> 00:26:02,584
No way.

406
00:26:02,728 --> 00:26:06,460
You're sure you didn't see Judge
Eller around 11:00 that night?

407
00:26:06,598 --> 00:26:09,329
I'm positive. My girlfriend's
got to be home by 10:30.

408
00:26:09,468 --> 00:26:10,561
Oh.

409
00:26:12,037 --> 00:26:13,037
Thank you.

410
00:26:13,105 --> 00:26:15,233
Your Honor, may we approach?

411
00:26:15,374 --> 00:26:16,740
Come forward.

412
00:26:21,747 --> 00:26:23,978
Your Honor, we've just come
into photographic evidence

413
00:26:24,116 --> 00:26:26,142
that further incriminates
the defendant.

414
00:26:26,285 --> 00:26:29,016
The state would like to
reopen its case in chief.

415
00:26:29,154 --> 00:26:30,349
My chambers, gentlemen.

416
00:26:31,390 --> 00:26:33,052
Court's in recess.

417
00:26:41,300 --> 00:26:43,166
Where did these come from?

418
00:26:43,302 --> 00:26:46,033
They were left on my secretary's
desk about an hour ago.

419
00:26:46,171 --> 00:26:49,733
This is clearly the defendant, dressed
precisely the way Miss Lewan described.

420
00:26:49,875 --> 00:26:51,309
Not good enough, counselor.

421
00:26:51,443 --> 00:26:54,022
If Miss Lewan were in these
pictures, that would be another matter.

422
00:26:54,046 --> 00:26:57,312
But as it stands, at best
these photos show propensity.

423
00:26:57,449 --> 00:26:59,680
But because we cannot
verify their source,

424
00:26:59,818 --> 00:27:01,684
we therefore cannot
even say with certainty

425
00:27:01,820 --> 00:27:04,756
that the man they depict
is, in fact, Arthur Eller.

426
00:27:04,890 --> 00:27:07,792
- Your Honor...
- They're inadmissible, Mr. Sadowsky.

427
00:27:07,926 --> 00:27:09,588
End of discussion.

428
00:27:17,402 --> 00:27:19,303
Has the jury reached a verdict?

429
00:27:19,438 --> 00:27:21,202
- We have, Your Honor.
- What is the verdict?

430
00:27:21,340 --> 00:27:23,571
We, the members of
the Fulton County Jury,

431
00:27:23,709 --> 00:27:26,770
find the defendant, Judge
Arthur Eller, not guilty.

432
00:27:28,814 --> 00:27:30,146
Thank you, ladies and gentlemen.

433
00:27:30,282 --> 00:27:33,309
You are excused, and
this court is adjourned.

434
00:27:35,454 --> 00:27:39,755
Ben, uh, thank you.

435
00:27:39,892 --> 00:27:44,990
You bribed that boy or
blackmailed him or threatened him.

436
00:27:45,130 --> 00:27:48,760
Somehow you got him
to lie for you, didn't you?

437
00:27:48,901 --> 00:27:50,563
You weren't up at Athens Lake.

438
00:27:50,702 --> 00:27:53,262
You were at your
house that night,

439
00:27:53,405 --> 00:27:56,398
doing exactly what that
girl said you were doing.

440
00:28:12,624 --> 00:28:14,183
<i>Thanks for being
with us this morning.</i>

441
00:28:14,326 --> 00:28:15,954
What is that? Geraldo?

442
00:28:16,094 --> 00:28:18,653
<i>Stay tuned for a
special edition...</i> No.

443
00:28:18,797 --> 00:28:24,759
It's the local news interviewing some of
the people on the Brodsky commission...

444
00:28:24,903 --> 00:28:27,737
The one your rootin'-tootin'
cowboy friend is on.

445
00:28:27,873 --> 00:28:29,865
Arthur Eller is not
my friend, Dad.

446
00:28:30,008 --> 00:28:32,705
No one hates what he did to
that poor girl more than I do.

447
00:28:32,844 --> 00:28:35,177
I sure wish you
hadn't talked me into it.

448
00:28:35,314 --> 00:28:36,714
Dad, I got your point, okay?

449
00:28:36,848 --> 00:28:38,373
Hello!

450
00:28:38,517 --> 00:28:39,576
In here.

451
00:28:45,257 --> 00:28:47,283
Did I come at a bad time?

452
00:28:47,426 --> 00:28:49,520
Oh, no. We're just
discussing the Eller case.

453
00:28:49,661 --> 00:28:51,152
Hush, they're
starting. Sit down.

454
00:28:51,296 --> 00:28:53,265
<i>Welcome to</i> Fulton County Forum.

455
00:28:53,398 --> 00:28:54,866
<i>I'm Dale Lambert.</i>

456
00:28:55,000 --> 00:28:58,300
<i>And this morning, I have with me three
members from the Brodsky Commission.</i>

457
00:28:58,437 --> 00:29:00,081
<i>That's the commission
formed to investigate</i>

458
00:29:00,105 --> 00:29:02,768
<i>the assassination last
month of Mayor Jeffries.</i>

459
00:29:02,908 --> 00:29:06,606
<i>On the left, I have
Police Chief Colin Young.</i>

460
00:29:06,745 --> 00:29:08,771
<i>In the center,
Judge Arthur Eller.</i>

461
00:29:08,914 --> 00:29:11,349
<i>And on the right,
Councilman Leo Brodsky.</i>

462
00:29:11,483 --> 00:29:14,248
<i>Gentlemen, thank you for taking
time out of your busy schedules</i>

463
00:29:14,386 --> 00:29:16,066
<i>- to be with me this morning.
- A pleasure.</i>

464
00:29:16,154 --> 00:29:18,589
<i>Judge Eller, that
trial must have been</i>

465
00:29:18,613 --> 00:29:21,525
<i>particularly hectic and
embarrassing for you.</i>

466
00:29:21,660 --> 00:29:24,061
<i>Yes, but now it's
over, I'm happy to say,</i>

467
00:29:24,196 --> 00:29:28,998
<i>and I'm very grateful to the people and
to the legal system that vindicated me.</i>

468
00:29:29,134 --> 00:29:32,332
<i>And I understand that the
investigation is still ongoing,</i>

469
00:29:32,471 --> 00:29:34,997
<i>but can you give us some idea
of what your findings are so far?</i>

470
00:29:35,140 --> 00:29:37,905
<i>So far, everything indicates
Jay Reynolds acted alone.</i>

471
00:29:38,043 --> 00:29:39,170
<i>Now, Colin.</i>

472
00:29:39,311 --> 00:29:40,404
<i>Jay Reynolds lost his job.</i>

473
00:29:40,545 --> 00:29:42,377
<i>He blamed the
mayor, so he shot him.</i>

474
00:29:42,514 --> 00:29:44,847
<i>- What about the other man?
- Because he was in the way.</i>

475
00:29:44,983 --> 00:29:46,227
<i>Then why did he
shoot him second?</i>

476
00:29:46,251 --> 00:29:48,162
<i>Come on, gentlemen.
We've all seen the videotape.</i>

477
00:29:48,186 --> 00:29:50,698
<i>You're referring to the videotape, of
course, that was shot by the tourist?</i>

478
00:29:50,722 --> 00:29:52,802
<i>- Yes, that's right.
- Jay Reynolds was paid.</i>

479
00:29:52,858 --> 00:29:54,827
<i>- Ah, come on.</i>
- They hate you, Arthur.

480
00:29:54,960 --> 00:29:56,920
<i>- There is not one shred of...</i>
- Get him. Get him.

481
00:29:56,995 --> 00:29:59,396
- Dad.
<i>- Evidence... There is not a shred...</i>

482
00:30:00,565 --> 00:30:02,625
And we'll see you
again next week.

483
00:30:02,768 --> 00:30:04,430
And we're clear.

484
00:30:04,569 --> 00:30:06,162
Thank you, gentlemen.

485
00:30:06,305 --> 00:30:08,774
That certainly was a
spirited show, to say the least.

486
00:30:12,077 --> 00:30:13,636
You fellows were great.

487
00:30:13,779 --> 00:30:16,078
Especially you, darling.

488
00:30:16,214 --> 00:30:17,910
Thank you, my dear.

489
00:30:18,050 --> 00:30:19,279
Mrs. Brodsky.

490
00:30:20,419 --> 00:30:22,388
See you both in the
hearing room at 1.

491
00:30:22,521 --> 00:30:24,080
Chief Young.

492
00:30:24,222 --> 00:30:26,020
Judge Eller.

493
00:30:45,544 --> 00:30:47,638
I'm afraid you were
right, Mr. Brodsky.

494
00:31:01,326 --> 00:31:02,350
No.

495
00:31:03,562 --> 00:31:05,554
His name is Arthur Eller.

496
00:31:07,599 --> 00:31:10,660
I... I don't believe this.

497
00:31:13,705 --> 00:31:14,705
I'll send you a bill.

498
00:31:14,773 --> 00:31:18,239
Ohh... Are you going to be okay?

499
00:31:22,547 --> 00:31:24,175
I'm going to kill her.

500
00:31:25,450 --> 00:31:26,474
I'm going to kill her!

501
00:31:26,618 --> 00:31:27,950
Whoa! Hey, take it easy.

502
00:31:50,709 --> 00:31:52,371
How could you
do this to me, Eva?

503
00:31:52,511 --> 00:31:54,446
I loved you.

504
00:32:00,519 --> 00:32:01,987
What are you doing?

505
00:32:05,090 --> 00:32:08,083
Oh my God. What have you done?

506
00:32:20,372 --> 00:32:22,739
I'd like to report a shooting.

507
00:32:34,820 --> 00:32:36,083
Les? What are you doing?

508
00:32:36,221 --> 00:32:37,221
Hi, Ben.

509
00:32:37,355 --> 00:32:39,187
I'm just returning
your weed-whacker.

510
00:32:39,324 --> 00:32:41,122
And thanks. It
whacked like a charm.

511
00:32:41,259 --> 00:32:42,784
Oh, good. Come on in the house.

512
00:32:42,928 --> 00:32:47,059
Well, I don't want to keep
you. I know you're busy.

513
00:32:47,199 --> 00:32:48,199
Well...

514
00:32:49,701 --> 00:32:51,829
Is Leigh Anne still here?

515
00:32:51,970 --> 00:32:54,030
Yeah. She'll be here for
a couple of more days.

516
00:32:54,172 --> 00:32:55,435
Oh, that's a shame.

517
00:32:55,574 --> 00:32:56,872
Why?

518
00:32:57,008 --> 00:32:59,671
Well, when I first met
her... First, mind you...

519
00:32:59,811 --> 00:33:02,337
She started getting
nosy about my love life.

520
00:33:02,481 --> 00:33:04,848
She did? Huh.

521
00:33:04,983 --> 00:33:08,476
Well, maybe she was curious.

522
00:33:08,620 --> 00:33:11,215
Maybe she heard
about your history.

523
00:33:11,356 --> 00:33:13,752
Well... Daddy?

524
00:33:13,892 --> 00:33:15,451
Well, I'll see you.

525
00:33:18,630 --> 00:33:20,531
- Morning, Pop.
- Mmm.

526
00:33:21,633 --> 00:33:23,261
- Coffee ready?
- Right there.

527
00:33:25,136 --> 00:33:28,368
Les just left in the
middle of a conversation.

528
00:33:28,507 --> 00:33:30,271
What were y'all taking about?

529
00:33:30,408 --> 00:33:33,344
I don't know...
something about women.

530
00:33:35,313 --> 00:33:38,477
If he wasn't 20 years
too old for a mid-life crisis,

531
00:33:38,617 --> 00:33:40,882
I'd swear he was
having hot flashes.

532
00:33:42,954 --> 00:33:45,685
Did you get on to
him about women?

533
00:33:46,825 --> 00:33:50,785
Well, he got on to me about my personal
life, so I got on to him about his.

534
00:33:50,929 --> 00:33:53,626
You know all about
that, don't you, Dad?

535
00:33:53,765 --> 00:33:55,461
Leigh Anne.

536
00:34:03,775 --> 00:34:05,141
Ben Matlock.

537
00:34:08,079 --> 00:34:09,411
Oh.

538
00:34:10,549 --> 00:34:15,379
Oh... Okay. I'll be
there in a minute.

539
00:34:16,888 --> 00:34:18,083
What's up?

540
00:34:23,795 --> 00:34:26,629
Are you nuts, Leo, or what?

541
00:34:26,765 --> 00:34:30,031
Well, when I saw the pictures
of them together, I just lost it.

542
00:34:30,168 --> 00:34:31,466
I went crazy.

543
00:34:31,603 --> 00:34:35,040
This used to be
such a nice little town.

544
00:34:35,173 --> 00:34:38,610
I know this doesn't make any
sense, but I loved my wife, Ben.

545
00:34:38,743 --> 00:34:40,609
I didn't mean to kill her.

546
00:34:40,745 --> 00:34:42,577
Ohh. What a mess.

547
00:34:42,714 --> 00:34:43,807
What should I do?

548
00:34:45,116 --> 00:34:47,711
Oh, pray.

549
00:34:50,355 --> 00:34:52,415
- Good morning, Ben.
- Hello, Bob.

550
00:34:53,725 --> 00:34:56,661
Well, we dropped the
charges. You're free to go.

551
00:34:56,795 --> 00:34:57,795
What?

552
00:34:57,862 --> 00:35:00,525
According to the coroner, your
wife was dead before you shot her.

553
00:35:00,665 --> 00:35:01,689
Cyanide poisoning.

554
00:35:01,833 --> 00:35:03,165
Suicide?

555
00:35:03,301 --> 00:35:05,566
We don't know for sure
yet, but there was no note,

556
00:35:05,704 --> 00:35:10,938
and we found a cup containing traces
of cyanide-laced tea in the bedroom.

557
00:35:11,076 --> 00:35:12,916
It had Arthur Eller's
fingerprints all over it.

558
00:35:34,032 --> 00:35:38,265
Well, are you going
to go or just sit there?

559
00:35:38,403 --> 00:35:41,237
I'm not going to be
charged with anything?

560
00:35:41,373 --> 00:35:42,397
No. No.

561
00:35:42,540 --> 00:35:47,274
They'll probably fine you
for defacing the corpse.

562
00:35:47,412 --> 00:35:49,745
But I did try to kill her.

563
00:35:49,881 --> 00:35:51,850
I know, but she
was already dead.

564
00:35:53,852 --> 00:35:55,184
I don't understand.

565
00:35:57,956 --> 00:36:01,449
It's factually an impossibility.

566
00:36:01,593 --> 00:36:07,089
You can't take the life of someone
if there's no life for you to take.

567
00:36:07,232 --> 00:36:08,232
Well, I...

568
00:36:08,333 --> 00:36:11,235
If it were up to me, I'd
throw you out of the country.

569
00:36:11,369 --> 00:36:14,498
But the truth is your case
would be thrown out of court.

570
00:36:14,639 --> 00:36:18,576
Now, stay if you
like, but I'm leaving.

571
00:36:27,719 --> 00:36:30,746
♪ Whoa, mule,
whoa, mule, I say ♪

572
00:36:30,889 --> 00:36:32,414
♪ Grab your hat,
Miss Lucy Jane ♪

573
00:36:32,557 --> 00:36:33,923
♪ And hold on to the shay ♪

574
00:36:34,926 --> 00:36:37,623
Mm. Whoo-hoo! Mm.

575
00:36:40,765 --> 00:36:42,199
Tomato sauce?

576
00:36:42,333 --> 00:36:44,632
Are you making
meatballs and spaghetti?

577
00:36:44,769 --> 00:36:45,862
Special recipe.

578
00:36:46,004 --> 00:36:47,700
Oh, that is so sweet of you.

579
00:36:50,575 --> 00:36:53,067
Mm-mm-mm-mm-mm-mm-mm.

580
00:37:02,887 --> 00:37:03,887
What?

581
00:37:03,988 --> 00:37:05,422
It needed more oregano.

582
00:37:05,557 --> 00:37:07,389
No, it didn't.

583
00:37:07,525 --> 00:37:09,357
Oh, I've offended you.

584
00:37:09,494 --> 00:37:11,224
You didn't offend me.

585
00:37:11,362 --> 00:37:13,661
- You know where I got this recipe?
- Hm-mm.

586
00:37:13,798 --> 00:37:17,667
Straight from the mouth of the
owner of a five-star restaurant.

587
00:37:17,802 --> 00:37:22,001
I asked him, "What's the
secret to great spaghetti sauce?"

588
00:37:22,140 --> 00:37:24,180
- You know what he said?
- No, Dad. What did he say?

589
00:37:24,209 --> 00:37:26,269
Go easy on the oregano.

590
00:37:30,815 --> 00:37:31,815
Hmm.

591
00:37:39,524 --> 00:37:41,891
- Ben, I need your help.
- Get off my porch.

592
00:37:42,026 --> 00:37:44,371
- I don't blame you. I lied to you.
- You're damn right you did.

593
00:37:44,395 --> 00:37:45,795
But I didn't kill Eva Brodsky.

594
00:37:45,930 --> 00:37:47,523
Garbage! You were
sleeping with her.

595
00:37:47,665 --> 00:37:49,410
Yeah, I slept with
her, but I didn't kill her.

596
00:37:49,434 --> 00:37:51,198
Somebody is trying to frame me.

597
00:37:51,336 --> 00:37:52,497
Get out of here.

598
00:37:54,372 --> 00:37:55,533
Stay right there.

599
00:37:58,343 --> 00:38:00,543
In my opinion, there's a
good chance he didn't kill her.

600
00:38:00,612 --> 00:38:01,944
Oh, come on, Leigh Anne.

601
00:38:02,080 --> 00:38:04,640
I know, Dad. He's a womanizer
and a pervert and a liar.

602
00:38:04,783 --> 00:38:06,360
- You got that right.
- But a smart liar.

603
00:38:06,384 --> 00:38:09,980
If he had killed her, he'd make
sure he had three alibis, all airtight.

604
00:38:10,121 --> 00:38:11,350
Come on, Leigh Anne.

605
00:38:11,489 --> 00:38:13,048
You won't take his case?

606
00:38:14,159 --> 00:38:18,824
No... no matter how many
times you quote things I've said.

607
00:38:18,963 --> 00:38:20,898
All right. That's your right.

608
00:38:25,170 --> 00:38:26,866
Would you come in?

609
00:38:37,949 --> 00:38:40,885
I don't care what you say.

610
00:38:41,019 --> 00:38:43,079
You took the case for spite.

611
00:38:43,221 --> 00:38:44,985
I did not take
the case for spite.

612
00:38:45,123 --> 00:38:46,216
Then why did you take it?

613
00:38:46,357 --> 00:38:47,586
I don't think he did it.

614
00:38:47,725 --> 00:38:51,423
And the worst person in the
world deserves representation.

615
00:38:51,563 --> 00:38:53,327
I don't know about that.

616
00:38:53,464 --> 00:38:55,330
He already lied to me.

617
00:38:55,466 --> 00:38:57,901
He was sleeping
with Eva Brodsky.

618
00:38:58,036 --> 00:38:59,834
And you took it for spite.

619
00:38:59,971 --> 00:39:02,964
Dad, we've grown apart.

620
00:39:03,107 --> 00:39:06,600
We are alike and yet so different,
and gradually we've grown apart.

621
00:39:06,744 --> 00:39:10,647
I know it's hard for you to
think of me as an adult, but I am.

622
00:39:10,782 --> 00:39:13,445
I know this Arthur
Eller is low and base,

623
00:39:13,585 --> 00:39:18,148
but something about the case intrigues
me, and you've got to let me have my head.

624
00:39:18,289 --> 00:39:20,952
Some things don't have
simple explanations.

625
00:39:23,261 --> 00:39:25,196
Everything I know does.

626
00:39:25,330 --> 00:39:27,060
- Yeah?
- Yeah.

627
00:39:27,198 --> 00:39:29,360
When I was 5, I asked you
where babies came from,

628
00:39:29,500 --> 00:39:30,991
and you said, "Go
ask your mother."

629
00:39:31,135 --> 00:39:32,296
Well, yeah, but...

630
00:39:32,437 --> 00:39:34,030
Things were difficult for her.

631
00:39:34,172 --> 00:39:36,607
She told me about God and
that babies came from heaven.

632
00:39:36,741 --> 00:39:38,403
Well, they do.

633
00:39:38,543 --> 00:39:39,704
Where are you going?

634
00:39:39,844 --> 00:39:41,676
Room 335.

635
00:39:41,813 --> 00:39:44,373
I'm meeting whoever's
prosecuting the case for discovery.

636
00:39:44,515 --> 00:39:46,040
Mind if I go with you?

637
00:39:46,184 --> 00:39:47,618
Why?

638
00:39:47,752 --> 00:39:49,935
And here's the
report filed by the

639
00:39:49,959 --> 00:39:52,816
detective who searched
Arthur Eller's house,

640
00:39:52,957 --> 00:39:59,022
during which a container of cyanide
was found in a cupboard beneath his sink.

641
00:40:05,370 --> 00:40:07,965
Well, that doesn't
make sense, does it?

642
00:40:08,106 --> 00:40:11,099
He dumps cyanide
in his lover's tea,

643
00:40:11,242 --> 00:40:14,610
then runs home past hundreds
of thousands of garbage cans

644
00:40:14,746 --> 00:40:19,013
so that he can put the incriminating
evidence in plain sight under his sink.

645
00:40:19,150 --> 00:40:22,382
Also, the police tracked
down the messenger

646
00:40:22,520 --> 00:40:24,284
who left those
photos of your client

647
00:40:24,422 --> 00:40:28,382
dressed... or should I say
undressed... As a cowboy.

648
00:40:28,526 --> 00:40:33,624
And, according to his statement, he
received a phone call from a woman

649
00:40:33,765 --> 00:40:36,860
telling him to pick up the
envelope containing the photos

650
00:40:37,001 --> 00:40:41,496
from a house at 1128 North
Star Drive... Eva Brodsky's house.

651
00:40:41,639 --> 00:40:43,403
Eva Brodsky sent those pictures?

652
00:40:43,541 --> 00:40:48,172
My guess is that she and Judge
Eller had a fight or something,

653
00:40:48,313 --> 00:40:51,283
and she sent them in
order to get revenge,

654
00:40:51,416 --> 00:40:53,885
only he found out, got
mad, and killed her...

655
00:40:54,018 --> 00:40:55,816
Which gives us motive.

656
00:40:55,954 --> 00:40:58,287
Did the messenger
actually see Eva Brodsky

657
00:40:58,423 --> 00:41:00,134
when he picked up the
photos at that address?

658
00:41:00,158 --> 00:41:03,094
No, she left them
for him in the mailbox.

659
00:41:03,227 --> 00:41:05,219
Anybody could
have put them there.

660
00:41:05,363 --> 00:41:09,733
And finally, I have the statement
of one Curtis Bartholomew,

661
00:41:09,867 --> 00:41:12,393
who lives across the street
from the Brodsky residence.

662
00:41:12,537 --> 00:41:14,631
And he's ready to testify

663
00:41:14,772 --> 00:41:20,302
that he saw Arthur Eller enter
Eva's house at approximately 5 p.m.

664
00:41:20,445 --> 00:41:22,209
the day she was murdered.

665
00:41:22,347 --> 00:41:23,940
He said he wasn't there all day.

666
00:41:33,725 --> 00:41:36,024
Okay, well, he probably
did see me there.

667
00:41:36,160 --> 00:41:38,959
Eva called, said that Leo wasn't
going to be home for dinner.

668
00:41:39,097 --> 00:41:42,864
I went there straight after
work, stayed till about 6:30.

669
00:41:43,001 --> 00:41:44,970
You lied to me, Judge Eller.

670
00:41:46,237 --> 00:41:47,728
Leigh Anne, I'm a judge.

671
00:41:47,872 --> 00:41:50,706
I've presided over
literally hundreds of cases.

672
00:41:50,842 --> 00:41:55,075
If the jury had found out that I
went to Eva's house that night,

673
00:41:55,213 --> 00:41:57,011
that's all they'd remember.

674
00:41:57,148 --> 00:41:58,548
I had no choice but to lie.

675
00:41:58,683 --> 00:42:01,016
What else have you lied about?

676
00:42:02,553 --> 00:42:04,886
Nothing. That's it. I swear.

677
00:42:05,023 --> 00:42:07,117
Let's get this
straight, Judge Eller.

678
00:42:07,258 --> 00:42:12,287
You lie to me once more about this
case, about what you had for breakfast,

679
00:42:12,430 --> 00:42:15,798
about anything,
and I'll be history.

680
00:42:15,933 --> 00:42:19,927
And believe me, so will you.

681
00:42:21,706 --> 00:42:22,935
You got that?

682
00:42:24,108 --> 00:42:25,303
Understood.

683
00:42:26,377 --> 00:42:28,903
Now, where is this
videotape you want me to see?

684
00:42:30,114 --> 00:42:34,882
Somebody sitting at the cafe
recorded this thing from start to finish.

685
00:42:35,019 --> 00:42:38,512
Well, this is all very interesting, Judge,
but what's it got to do with your case?

686
00:42:38,656 --> 00:42:41,148
I was the only person on
the Brodsky commission

687
00:42:41,292 --> 00:42:44,751
who believed that Jay Reynolds
shot the mayor because he was paid.

688
00:42:44,896 --> 00:42:48,458
That's why I've been framed, because
somebody wants me off that commission.

689
00:42:48,599 --> 00:42:50,359
Okay, now here's what
I want you to look at.

690
00:42:50,435 --> 00:42:53,928
Right... there.

691
00:42:54,072 --> 00:42:56,371
See that guy standing
there in the jean jacket?

692
00:42:56,507 --> 00:42:58,169
- Mm-hmm.
- He stands there, he waits.

693
00:42:58,309 --> 00:43:02,076
If I rolled the tape, you'd see
that he starts running around

694
00:43:02,213 --> 00:43:04,409
like he's freaked
out or something,

695
00:43:04,549 --> 00:43:07,383
but if he were really freaked
out, you'd think he'd leave.

696
00:43:07,518 --> 00:43:09,282
Watch what he does.

697
00:43:11,456 --> 00:43:15,860
Okay, he's out of frame for a few
minutes with all this general confusion.

698
00:43:15,993 --> 00:43:18,462
Then, when he re-enters,
who does he run into?

699
00:43:18,596 --> 00:43:20,963
The killer Jay Reynolds.

700
00:43:22,166 --> 00:43:25,500
It's almost as if he knew Jay
Reynolds was coming out of that building

701
00:43:25,636 --> 00:43:27,832
and wanted to make
sure he got caught.

702
00:43:27,972 --> 00:43:29,804
That's right.

703
00:43:29,941 --> 00:43:33,639
Then he disappears into the crowd
before the police can take his name.

704
00:43:33,778 --> 00:43:36,213
The man's never heard again.

705
00:43:36,347 --> 00:43:37,713
I wonder who he is.

706
00:43:37,849 --> 00:43:39,442
Yeah.

707
00:43:55,433 --> 00:43:57,026
What do you want?

708
00:43:57,168 --> 00:43:59,660
We've got a little problem.

709
00:43:59,804 --> 00:44:02,137
Nothing you and
Judd can't handle.

710
00:44:16,320 --> 00:44:17,618
No, no, no, Dad.

711
00:44:17,755 --> 00:44:19,833
I distinctly remember, he
wasn't on <i>The Beverly Hillbillies.</i>

712
00:44:19,857 --> 00:44:21,052
He was on <i>Green Acres.</i>

713
00:44:21,192 --> 00:44:23,991
Leigh Anne, we used to
watch those shows together.

714
00:44:24,128 --> 00:44:25,528
It was <i>The Beverly Hillbillies.</i>

715
00:44:25,663 --> 00:44:28,462
Dad, the pig was on <i>Green
Acres.</i> He even won an award.

716
00:44:28,599 --> 00:44:31,467
The pig was on <i>Beverly
Hillbillies,</i> and his name was Jed.

717
00:44:31,602 --> 00:44:34,071
His name was Arnold,
and he was on <i>Green Acres.</i>

718
00:44:34,205 --> 00:44:35,366
Who was Jed?

719
00:44:35,506 --> 00:44:37,975
Jed Clampett... Buddy
Ebsen, <i>Beverly Hillbillies.</i>

720
00:44:38,109 --> 00:44:40,442
Oh, yeah. That's
right. I remember now.

721
00:44:40,578 --> 00:44:44,015
Every time we watched <i>Green
Acres,</i> I got hungry for a barbecue.

722
00:44:45,049 --> 00:44:46,176
I'll make some tea.

723
00:45:32,163 --> 00:45:33,373
<i>I agree with him, Dad.</i>

724
00:45:33,397 --> 00:45:34,397
How'd I do?

725
00:45:34,532 --> 00:45:36,176
<i>I think he was framed
because of that commission.</i>

726
00:45:36,200 --> 00:45:37,862
Mr. Taylor, you're a genius.

727
00:45:38,002 --> 00:45:39,231
<i>Unless he's lying.</i>

728
00:45:39,370 --> 00:45:41,100
<i>This time, I don't think so.</i>

729
00:45:53,084 --> 00:45:56,282
You say this man paid
you to kill the mayor?

730
00:45:56,420 --> 00:45:58,082
What did he look like?

731
00:45:59,924 --> 00:46:03,486
Oh, I don't know... medium
height, medium build, brown hair.

732
00:46:03,628 --> 00:46:05,187
I only met him twice.

733
00:46:05,329 --> 00:46:07,798
- Where did you meet him?
- Parking garage at the mall.

734
00:46:07,932 --> 00:46:10,299
I'd park in a particular
spot, and he'd walk up.

735
00:46:10,434 --> 00:46:12,801
We'd talk, he'd walk away.

736
00:46:12,937 --> 00:46:14,963
The second time,
he gave me 25 grand

737
00:46:15,106 --> 00:46:20,010
and told me that the mayor would be
at the Shun Lee Cafe at 3 that afternoon.

738
00:46:20,144 --> 00:46:22,909
He told me to take out
him and whoever he met.

739
00:46:23,047 --> 00:46:24,538
Did he tell you why?

740
